L219: # VeriSoftBench
L220: 
L221: A benchmark for evaluating neural theorem provers on software verification tasks in Lean 4.
L222: 
L223: VeriSoftBench contains 500 theorem-proving tasks drawn from 23 real-world Lean 4 repositories spanning compiler verification, type system formalization, applied verification (zero-knowledge proofs, smart contracts), semantic frameworks, and more.
L224: 
L225: cite102†Paper†arxiv.org L226: 
L227: cite103†Huggingface Dataset†huggingface.co L228: ## Quick Start
L229: 
L230: ### 1. Install dependencies
L231: 
L232:     pip install -r requirements.txt
L233: 
L234: ### 2. Get the dataset
L235: 
L236: The dataset (`data/verisoftbench.jsonl`) is included in this repository.
L237: ### 3. Build Lean repositories
L238: 
L239: Proofs are verified against the actual Lean repositories. You need either Docker (recommended) or a local build.
L240: 
L241: Option A: Docker (recommended)
L242: 
L243:     # Build the image (~1 hour, downloads Mathlib caches)
L244:     docker build -t verisoftbench/lean:latest .
L245: 
L246:     # Start the container
L247:     docker run -d --name verisoftbench-test verisoftbench/lean:latest
L248: 
L249: Option B: Local build
L250: Requires cite104†elan (the Lean version manager, which provides `lake`):
L251: 
L252:     curl -sSf https://raw.githubusercontent.com/leanprover/elan/master/elan-init.sh | sh
L253:     ./scripts/setup_repos.sh --clone --config-dir build_config --output-dir data/lean_repos
L254: ### 4. Run evaluation
L255: 
L256:     # With a config file (recommended)
L257:     python evaluate.py --config configs/openai_example.yaml
L258: 
L259:     # Or with CLI arguments
L260:     python evaluate.py \
L261:       --model-name openai \
L262:       --model-id gpt-4o \
L263:       --lean-backend docker \
L264:       --task-ids 1:10
L265: 
L266: ## Configuration
L267: ### Config files
L268: 
L269: YAML config files in `configs/` are the recommended way to configure runs. Config values override CLI arguments.
L270: 
L271: File  | Description
L272: --- | ---
L273: `configs/default.yaml`  | Full reference config with all options documented
L274: `configs/openai_example.yaml`  | OpenAI (GPT-4o)
L275: `configs/claude_example.yaml`  | Anthropic (Claude)
L276: `configs/gemini_example.yaml`  | Google (Gemini)
L277: ### API keys
L278: 
L279: Set your API key via environment variable (preferred) or in the config file:
L280: 
L281:     # OpenAI
L282:     export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
L283: 
L284:     # Anthropic
L285:     export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
L286: 
L287:     # Google
L288:     export GOOGLE_API_KEY=...
L289: ### Key options
L290: Option  | Default  | Description
L291: --- | --- | ---
L292: `model_name`  | `openai`  | Provider: `openai`, `claude`, or `gemini`
L293: `model_id`  | `gpt-4o`  | Model identifier
L294: `mode`  | `filtered_context`  | Context mode (see below)
L295: `fix_enabled`  | `true`  | Enable proof fixing (3 rounds)
L296: `num_samples`  | `1`  | Proof attempts per theorem
L297: `max_workers`  | `8`  | Parallel evaluation workers
L298: `lean_backend`  | `local`  | `local` or `docker`
L299: `task_ids`  | all  | Filter tasks: `"1:10"` (range) or `"1,5,10"` (list)
L300: `category`  | all  | Filter by category (exact match)
L301: `refresh_cache`  | `false`  | Re-evaluate cached tasks
L302: ### Context modes
L303: 
L304:   * `filtered_context` (default): Each task gets only the definitions and lemmas identified as relevant by static analysis. This is the primary evaluation mode.
L305:   * `full_context`: Each task gets all definitions and lemmas from its repository. Useful for testing models with richer context.
L306:   * `full_context_limited`: Pre-generated full-context prompts for ArkLib and lean-mlir (the two largest repos). Avoids token limits by capping context size.
L307: ## Task selection
L308: 
L309:     # Evaluate specific task IDs
L310:     python evaluate.py --config configs/default.yaml --task-ids 1,5,10,15
L311: 
L312:     # Evaluate a range
L313:     python evaluate.py --config configs/default.yaml --task-ids 1:50
L314: 
L315:     # Evaluate a category (exact match on category field)
L316:     python evaluate.py --config configs/default.yaml --category "Transformation Correctness"
L317: 
L318:     # Evaluate a random subset
L319:     python evaluate.py --config configs/default.yaml --subset 50
L320: ## Results
L321: 
L322: Results are saved to `results/data/<run_name>/`:
L323: 
L324:     `results/data/gpt-4o_20250210_120000/
L325:       summary.json         # Overall statistics
L326:       details/             # Per-theorem results
L327:         theorem_name__file.json
L328:     `
L329: 
L330: `summary.json` contains:
L331: 
L332:     {
L333:       "total_theorems": 500,
L334:       "theorems_proved_wo_fix": 120,
L335:       "theorems_proved_w_fix": 145,
L336:       "proof_success_rate_wo_fix": 0.24,
L337:       "proof_success_rate_w_fix": 0.29
L338:     }
L339: Results are cached per model in `results/cache/`. Subsequent runs skip already-evaluated tasks unless `--refresh-cache` is set.
L340: ## Docker details
L341: The Docker image contains pre-built Lean repositories with all dependencies compiled. The evaluation pipeline runs on the host and uses `docker exec` to invoke Lean compilation inside the container.
L342: 
L343:     # Build (from the repository root)
L344:     docker build -t verisoftbench/lean:latest .
L345: 
L346:     # Start (stays running for docker exec calls)
L347:     docker run -d --name verisoftbench-test verisoftbench/lean:latest
L348: 
L349:     # Run evaluation against the container
L350:     python evaluate.py --config configs/openai_example.yaml --lean-backend docker
L351: 
L352:     # Stop when done
L353:     docker stop verisoftbench-test
L354: The image is ~110 GB and takes ~1 hour to build (Mathlib cache downloads dominate build time).
